<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Dahlak</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1557 CE (approx.) · capital Dahlak Kebir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An island sultanate in the Red Sea off Eritrea that controlled pearl fisheries and shipping between Egypt, Arabia and Ethiopia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dahlak archipelago was drawn into the Islamic world early, serving as a place of exile under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. By the 10th century a local Muslim dynasty ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahlak-kebir">Dahlak Kebir</a>, the largest island, controlling the sea approaches to the Ethiopian coast and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans grew rich from pearls, from customs on ships trading slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and hides from the African mainland, and from their position on the route to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Hundreds of Arabic tombstones from the 10th to 13th centuries record merchants, scholars and officials. The sultans often paid tribute to rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and dealt with Christian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> as both trade partner and rival.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The sultanate declined as trade shifted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and the islands in 1557, incorporating them into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a>. Its cemeteries and cisterns on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahlak-kebir">Dahlak Kebir</a> are among the main medieval Islamic sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sultanate-of-dahlak.jpg" alt="Sultanate of Dahlak" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: LeGabrie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Approximate_extension_of_the_Zagwe_Empire.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1557 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dahlak Kebir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dahlak Sultanate, Dahlak</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1254&amp;focus=sultanate-of-dahlak" aria-label="Sultanate of Dahlak on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5774 1595 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Dahlak lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6094 1851L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6094 1851h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6094 1851h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dahlak Kebir. <a href="/atlas?year=1254&amp;focus=sultanate-of-dahlak">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

